* report.i -- shared RESULT-line emitter for the OS-9/68000 conformance* suite (CONF68K). Pulled into every test with "use report.i" after the* test's own code and before its strings.** Every test writes exactly one line to path 1 (standard output), which* runall redirects into RESULTS/report:**   RESULT tNN VERDICT  obs=NNNNNN exp=NNNNNN  description** The line is assembled by a series of I$Write calls rather than into a* buffer. That is deliberate: the 6809 suite's report.i builds the line in* one 128-byte buffer and computes the length as (end - start), which works* but makes buffer size load-bearing -- too short a buffer silently* overwrites the variables stored after it. Writing each piece straight to* the path removes the buffer, and with it that whole failure mode. The* pieces arrive in order on the same path, so the line on disk is identical.** Nothing here depends on any Microware runtime: no C library, no trap* handler, no BASIC09. A test built from this needs only a kernel and a* shell, which is what lets the suite run on a system that has no* development tools installed at all.** Register use: every routine clobbers d0 and d1 (the syscall registers).* put6dec additionally clobbers d2, d3 and a1. No routine preserves a0.*IWrite   set $8AFExit    set $06** putstr -- a0 = address of a NUL-terminated string. Writes it to path 1* without the terminator. A zero-length string writes nothing and is not an* error.putstr movea.l a0,a1 moveq #0,d1psscan tst.b (a1)+ beq.s psgo addq.l #1,d1 bra.s psscanpsgo moveq #1,d0 trap #0 dc.w IWrite rts** putcr -- terminate the line. CR alone (0x0D), no LF: on OS-9 the line* terminator IS CR, and SCF adds the line feed itself on the way to a* terminal. Writing 13,10 here would put a stray byte in RESULTS/report on* every line, which the tally then has to step over.putcr lea crbyte(pc),a0 moveq #1,d1 moveq #1,d0 trap #0 dc.w IWrite rts** put6dec -- d2.l = value, written as exactly six zero-padded decimal* digits, so report lines stay diffable column-for-column.** Six digits, not five: 68k file positions are a full 32 bits (I$Seek takes* d1.l) and this suite's seek tests deliberately work above 65535, so a* five-digit field would silently truncate the very value they exist to* check. A value of 1000000 or more does not fit either, and is reported as* the literal string OVRFLW rather than as its own low six digits -- a* wrong number that looks plausible is worse than an obvious marker.put6dec sub.l #8,a7 movea.l a7,a1 adda.l #8,a1 moveq #0,d3 tst.l d2 bne.s p6loop move.b #'0',-(a1) moveq #1,d3 bra.s p6padp6loop tst.l d2 beq.s p6pad* 32-bit divide by 10 in two 16-bit steps. A bare DIVU.W overflows for any* dividend whose quotient needs more than 16 bits (>= 655360): it leaves* the destination untouched and sets only V, so large values print as* garbage. This split keeps both quotients in range on a plain 68000.* Lifted from test/68k-live-verification/batch8-01.a, already proven live. move.l d2,d0 clr.w d0 swap d0 divu.w #10,d0 move.w d2,d1 move.w d0,d2 swap d2 move.w d1,d0 divu.w #10,d0 move.w d0,d2 swap d0 move.b d0,d1 add.b #'0',d1 move.b d1,-(a1) addq.w #1,d3 bra.s p6loopp6pad cmpi.w #6,d3 bgt.s p6over cmpi.w #6,d3 beq.s p6out move.b #'0',-(a1) addq.w #1,d3 bra.s p6padp6out movea.l a1,a0 moveq #6,d1 moveq #1,d0 trap #0 dc.w IWrite add.l #8,a7 rtsp6over add.l #8,a7 lea ovrmsg(pc),a0 bsr putstr rts** finish -- normal termination, status 0. A test's verdict is the RESULT* line it printed, never its exit status: the shell does not record an exit* status into a redirected file, so a test that died silently would* otherwise be indistinguishable from one that passed. A missing RESULT* line is how the tally notices.finish moveq #0,d1 trap #0 dc.w FExit*crbyte dc.b 13ovrmsg dc.b "OVRFLW",0